## ***********************************************************************
## **
## **  APPHELP.INC.BL -- start an application politely, and leave it the
## **  way you found it. Plus panels saved to and loaded from disk.
## **
## **      APPHELP.STARTUP     remember the screen the user had
## **      APPHELP.RESTORE     put it back, on the way out
## **      APPHELP.PANEL.LOAD  and back again, where it came from
## **      APPHELP.PANEL.PUT   or somewhere else
## **
## **  WHY THIS IS NOT A KEYWORD. Everything here is composition -- one
## **  KERNAL call and some arithmetic -- and none of it runs in a loop or
## **  touches bulk data, so by the rule the whole GP set is built on it
## **  belongs in BASIC. It also means only programs that want it pay.
## **
## **  Usage:
## **      GOSUB APPHELP.STARTUP
## **      ... the application, laid out with APPHELP.COLS / APPHELP.ROWS
## **      GOSUB APPHELP.RESTORE : END
## **
## ***********************************************************************
#IFNDEF APPHELP.DEFS
#DEFINE APPHELP.DEFS 1

## The KERNAL's screen_mode. With CARRY SET it REPORTS: mode in .A, width
## in .X, height in .Y. With carry clear it SETS the mode in .A. GPC's own
## SCREEN command is the carry-clear half of exactly this call, which is
## also the proof the jump table is reachable from compiled code.
#DEFINE APPHELP.SCRMODE $FF5F

## The KERNAL's current text colour, packed (background * 16 + foreground)
## -- the same packing the GP drawing commands take, and the same byte
## they read when their colour argument is left off.
#DEFINE APPHELP.COLREG $0376

## Where a RAM bank appears, and the size of GP.STASH's self-describing
## header (w, h, x, y).

GOTO APPHELP.SKIP

## APPHELP.STARTUP -- remember what the user had, and report the size.
##
##   out  APPHELP.MODE    the screen mode, for APPHELP.RESTORE
##        APPHELP.COLS    columns, e.g. 80
##        APPHELP.ROWS    rows, e.g. 60
##        APPHELP.COLOUR  the packed text colour
##
## Call it FIRST, before any SCREEN or COLOR of your own -- it records the
## state as it finds it, so anything you change beforehand is what gets
## restored later.
##
## Lay the application out from APPHELP.COLS and APPHELP.ROWS rather than
## assuming 80x60. The X16 boots 80x60 but SCREEN 0 is 40x30, and a user
## who prefers the larger text will be running one.

APPHELP.STARTUP:
    GP.CALL APPHELP.SCRMODE, 0, 0, 0, 1
    APPHELP.MODE = GP.A
    APPHELP.COLS = GP.X
    APPHELP.ROWS = GP.Y
    APPHELP.COLOUR = PEEK(APPHELP.COLREG)
    RETURN

## APPHELP.RESTORE -- the screen as it was, for whatever runs next.
##
## Setting the mode CLEARS the screen, which is what you want on the way
## out: the user gets a clean READY rather than the wreckage of a dialog.
## Restoring the colour afterwards matters more than it looks -- leave it
## and every later PRINT, including BASIC's own, comes out in whatever the
## application last set.
##
## The two halves are split with INT and AND 15. AND is 16 bit SIGNED in
## GPC, so it is only safe here because a packed attribute is 0..255;
## above 32767 it raises OUT OF RANGE. See GP.STRPTR for where that bites.

APPHELP.RESTORE:
    GP.CALL APPHELP.SCRMODE, APPHELP.MODE, 0, 0, 0
    COLOR APPHELP.COLOUR AND 15, INT(APPHELP.COLOUR / 16)
    RETURN

## THE PANEL ROUTINES MOVED TO STASH.INC.BL on 2026-09-01, as
## STASH.FILE.SAVE, STASH.FILE.LOAD and STASH.FILE.PUT. They were
## APPHELP.PANEL.SAVE / .LOAD / .PUT, and they were built on GP.STASH and
## GP.RESTR -- which are gone, moved out of the GP runtime block into
## GP.ASM for the 329 bytes they cost every program that used any GP
## keyword at all. Keeping a BSAVE wrapper here would have meant APPHELP
## dragging the whole block in behind it.
##
APPHELP.SKIP:
#ENDIF
